1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,920
 Once a problem is clearly defined and scoped, the next step is turning that idea into a real

2
00:00:05,920 --> 00:00:12,800
 system. This is where many projects fail, not because the model is weak, but because the system

3
00:00:12,800 --> 00:00:20,240
 around it is poorly designed. AI does not live in isolation. It exists inside workflows,

4
00:00:20,240 --> 00:00:28,160
 APIs, user interfaces, and business rules. Before writing any code, you must translate the use case

5
00:00:28,160 --> 00:00:34,640
 into concrete components. Decide what the AI is responsible for and what should remain deterministic

6
00:00:34,640 --> 00:00:42,080
 logic. Identify inputs, outputs, and system boundaries early. This prevents scope creep and fragile

7
00:00:42,080 --> 00:00:48,960
 designs later. Skipping this step leads to brittle systems that are hard to debug, unsafe to scale,

8
00:00:48,960 --> 00:00:55,120
 and expensive to maintain. Architecture decisions made now directly impact reliability,

9
00:00:55,120 --> 00:01:03,120
 security, performance, and cost. Good system design makes implementation easier. Bad system design

10
00:01:03,120 --> 00:01:09,920
 guarantees rework. This slide sets the mindset. You are not building a prompt. You are building a

11
00:01:09,920 --> 00:01:17,360
 system. Every production AI system follows a layered architecture, even if it's not drawn explicitly.

12
00:01:17,360 --> 00:01:23,600
 Understanding these layers is critical for building something that survives real usage.

13
00:01:23,600 --> 00:01:30,560
 At the top is the user interface or API layer, where requests enter the system. Below that is

14
00:01:30,560 --> 00:01:36,400
 the orchestration layer, which handles routing, business rules, and workflow coordination. This

15
00:01:36,400 --> 00:01:43,520
 layer decides when to call the model, when to call tools, and when to escalate. The LLM interaction

16
00:01:43,520 --> 00:01:50,960
 layer manages prompt construction, model invocation, retries, and response parsing. Beneath it,

17
00:01:50,960 --> 00:01:57,680
 sit data sources, vector stores, databases, APIs, and external systems that provide context.

18
00:01:57,680 --> 00:02:04,880
 Finally, observability spans everything, logging, monitoring, alerts, and safety controls.

19
00:02:04,880 --> 00:02:12,080
 Without observability, failures go unnoticed. Clear separation of these layers makes systems

20
00:02:12,080 --> 00:02:19,280
 easier to reason about, test, and evolve. This architecture is the backbone of production grade AI

21
00:02:19,280 --> 00:02:25,040
 in production systems. Prompts are not experiments. They are engineering assets.

22
00:02:25,040 --> 00:02:30,160
 Treating prompts casually is one of the fastest ways to break reliability.

23
00:02:30,160 --> 00:02:37,040
 A strong prompt begins with a clear role definition. The model must know who it is and what it is

24
00:02:37,040 --> 00:02:44,640
 responsible for. Next, the expected output format must be explicit. Whether the output is JSON,

25
00:02:44,640 --> 00:02:48,800
 markdown, or text, ambiguity leads to downstream failures.

26
00:02:48,800 --> 00:02:55,840
 Constraints matter. Tone, length, and behavior boundaries should be defined upfront.

27
00:02:55,840 --> 00:03:02,480
 Prompts must also address ambiguity. What should the model do when information is missing or unclear?

28
00:03:02,480 --> 00:03:08,560
 Finally, prompts must be versioned. Store them in source control, review changes,

29
00:03:08,560 --> 00:03:15,200
 and enable rollback. This allows testing, iteration, and accountability. The key lesson,

30
00:03:15,200 --> 00:03:21,360
 prompts are code. If you wouldn't hot edit production code, you shouldn't hot edit production prompts.

31
00:03:21,360 --> 00:03:26,800
 The difference between experimental prompts and production prompts is structure.

32
00:03:26,800 --> 00:03:35,600
 Reliable systems demand predictability. System prompts define persistent behavior and constraints

33
00:03:35,600 --> 00:03:42,240
 that apply across all interactions. Step-based instructions break complex tasks

34
00:03:42,240 --> 00:03:48,480
 into sequential reasoning paths, reducing variance and outputs. Guard rails must be embedded

35
00:03:48,480 --> 00:03:53,360
 directly into the prompt, including refusal conditions and safety constraints.

36
00:03:53,360 --> 00:04:00,640
 Output schemas, especially JSON, allow responses to be parsed and validated programmatically

37
00:04:00,640 --> 00:04:07,440
 instead of guest. Finally, fallback logic is critical. The prompt should specify what to do

38
00:04:07,440 --> 00:04:13,920
 when confidence is low or inputs are invalid. Clear refusal and escalation instructions prevent

39
00:04:13,920 --> 00:04:20,560
 hallucination. Creative prompts may impress in demos. Structured prompts survive in production.

40
00:04:20,560 --> 00:04:28,480
 Structure doesn't limit capability. It enables reliability. Large language models do not know your

41
00:04:28,480 --> 00:04:34,560
 data. They rely on static training with knowledge cutoffs. Whenever accuracy matters,

42
00:04:34,560 --> 00:04:40,720
 memory is not enough. Retrieval augmented generation is essential when dealing with dynamic

43
00:04:40,720 --> 00:04:47,680
 information, proprietary data, or compliance sensitive content. Policies, documentation,

44
00:04:47,680 --> 00:04:52,400
 pricing, and internal knowledge must be retrieved at query time, not guest.

45
00:04:53,360 --> 00:05:00,160
 RAG is also mandatory when citations or audit trails are required. If hallucinations are unacceptable,

46
00:05:00,160 --> 00:05:06,400
 retrieval is non-negotiable. The decision rule is simple. Don't trust the model's memory.

47
00:05:06,400 --> 00:05:11,520
 If the information changes, belongs to your organization, or must be verifiable,

48
00:05:11,520 --> 00:05:17,360
 use retrieval. This slide teaches restraint. RAG isn't optional complexity,

49
00:05:17,360 --> 00:05:24,320
 its architectural responsibility. RAG systems follow a predictable life cycle. First,

50
00:05:24,320 --> 00:05:30,160
 documents are ingested and chunked into retrieval optimized segments. These chunks are converted

51
00:05:30,160 --> 00:05:36,960
 into embeddings and stored in a vector database. At query time, the user's question is embedded

52
00:05:36,960 --> 00:05:42,800
 and matched against the most relevant content. Retripped passages are injected into the prompt

53
00:05:42,800 --> 00:05:49,280
 as context, grounding the model's response. The model then generates an answer anchored to those

54
00:05:49,280 --> 00:05:56,240
 sources, often with citations. The model is not answering from memory. It is reasoning over

55
00:05:56,240 --> 00:06:03,520
 provided evidence. Understanding this flow helps debug failures, optimize retrieval quality,

56
00:06:03,520 --> 00:06:11,760
 and control hallucinations. Retrieval comes first. Generation comes second. That order matters.

57
00:06:11,760 --> 00:06:17,520
 Language models should not do everything. They are excellent at reasoning and language,

58
00:06:17,520 --> 00:06:23,840
 not precision tasks. Maths should be handled by computation engines. Live data should be

59
00:06:23,840 --> 00:06:31,520
 fetched via APIs. Structured queries should be executed using SQL, not natural language guessing.

60
00:06:31,520 --> 00:06:38,560
 The LLMs role is orchestration. It decides which tool to call and when, but it should not replace

61
00:06:38,560 --> 00:06:45,680
 deterministic systems. This design dramatically improves accuracy, reduces latency, and lowers

62
00:06:45,680 --> 00:06:54,000
 cost. It also makes failures easier to diagnose. Robust AI systems are hybrid systems, not model-only

63
00:06:54,000 --> 00:07:01,840
 solutions. Safety is not a feature you add later. It is an architectural decision. Input validation

64
00:07:01,840 --> 00:07:07,520
 filters prompt injection, malicious requests, and out-of-scope inputs before they reach the model.

65
00:07:08,160 --> 00:07:13,280
 Output filtering screens for harmful content, policy violations, and data leakage.

66
00:07:13,280 --> 00:07:18,400
 Role-based access ensures users only see what they are allowed to see.

67
00:07:18,400 --> 00:07:22,720
 Human and loop checkpoints enforce review for high-risk actions.

68
00:07:22,720 --> 00:07:29,680
 Finally, fail-safe defaults matter. When uncertain, systems should refuse rather than

69
00:07:29,680 --> 00:07:38,240
 hallucinate. Safety is infrastructure, not policy text. AI systems will fail. Planning for failure is a

70
00:07:38,240 --> 00:07:43,760
 sign of maturity. Confidence thresholds prevent low-quality answers from reaching users.

71
00:07:43,760 --> 00:07:51,360
 I don't know responses are better than confident errors. Escalation paths route complex cases to

72
00:07:51,360 --> 00:07:58,800
 humans. Failures should be logged, monitored, and surfaced quickly. User visible error handling builds

73
00:07:58,800 --> 00:08:05,680
 trust when things go wrong. Resilience systems don't pretend to be perfect. They fail safely,

74
00:08:05,680 --> 00:08:13,040
 visibly, and recoverably. Before implementation, pause. This checklist is your readiness gate.

75
00:08:13,040 --> 00:08:17,920
 You should have a documented system architecture, versioned prompts,

76
00:08:17,920 --> 00:08:24,800
 intentional RAG or tool decisions, active safety controls, and clearly defined failure behavior.

77
00:08:25,600 --> 00:08:31,440
 When these foundations are in place, implementation becomes execution, not discovery.

78
00:08:31,440 --> 00:08:38,240
 Architecture drives everything that follows. A well-designed system is easier to build,

79
00:08:38,240 --> 00:08:41,200
 easier to test, and harder to break.

